        "explanation": "Explanation:\nTest Case 1 : There are 444 possible pairs of sequences:\n\nX=(0),Y=(0)X = (0), Y = (0)X=(0),Y=(0) : X=YX = YX=Y already, so f(X,Y)=0f(X, Y) = 0f(X,Y)=0\nX=(1),Y=(0)X = (1), Y = (0)X=(1),Y=(0) : XXX cannot be made equal to YYY, so f(X,Y)=0f(X, Y) = 0f(X,Y)=0\nX=(0),Y=(1)X = (0), Y = (1)X=(0),Y=(1) : XXX cannot be made equal to YYY, so f(X,Y)=0f(X, Y) = 0f(X,Y)=0\nX=(1),Y=(1)X = (1), Y = (1)X=(1),Y=(1) : XXX is already equal to YYY, so f(X,Y)=0f(X, Y) = 0f(X,Y)=0\n\nTest Case 2 : There are 161616 total pairs of sequences. Out of that, only 222 pairs have a positive value:\n\nX=(0,1),Y=(1,0)X = (0, 1), Y = (1, 0)X=(0,1),Y=(1,0) : Choose i=1,j=2i = 1, j = 2i=1,j=2 and do the first action, swap X1X_1X1​ and X2X_2X2​, paying a cost of A=1A = 1A=1. f(X,Y)=1f(X, Y) = 1f(X,Y)=1.\nX=(1,0),Y=(0,1)X = (1, 0), Y = (0, 1)X=(1,0),Y=(0,1) : Choose i=1,j=2i = 1, j = 2i=1,j=2 and do the first action, swap X1X_1X1​ and X2X_2X2​, paying a cost of B=1B = 1B=1. f(X,Y)=1f(X, Y) = 1f(X,Y)=1.\n\nThus, the sum is 222."
